 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Index computation is $O(1)$ arithmetic operations (multiplications, additions, modulo) [S1, S4, S8, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Cubic with respect to resolution, $O(N^3)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels per axis [S7, S8, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** Provides the fastest possible query speed for dense data due to direct memory access without branching or tree traversal [S8, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Simple implementation, cache-friendly for contiguous access patterns (if spatial locality is preserved via SFC, though standard row-major has poor locality for non-adjacent slices [G]), deterministic indexing.
-- **Limitations:** Prohibitive memory usage for high-resolution grids or large bounding boxes; inefficient for sparse scenes where most voxels are empty [S7, S8, unofficial].
-- **When to Use:** Small to medium-sized scenes, dense volumes (e.g., fluid simulation, medical imaging with full resolution), or as a coarse approximation in multi-stage rendering (e.g., Direct Voxel Grid Optimization) [S10].
-- **When Not to Use:** Large open-world scenes with significant empty space, or when memory is constrained [S8, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Index computation is $O(1)$ arithmetic operations (multiplications, additions, modulo) [S8, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N^3)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels along one axis. This cubic growth makes high-resolution dense grids memory-prohibitive for large scenes [S7, S8, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Provides the fastest possible query latency due to direct array access without branching or tree traversal [S8, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, cache-friendly for contiguous access patterns, ideal for dense volumes or as a coarse approximation in hybrid methods [S8, unofficial][S10].
+*   **Limitations:** Wastes memory on empty space; resolution is limited by available VRAM/RAM; does not naturally handle multi-resolution or hierarchical detail [S7, S8, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Small to medium scenes, dense volumes, or as a coarse geometry proxy in neural rendering pipelines (e.g., Direct Voxel Grid Optimization) [S10].
+*   **When not to use:** Large-scale outdoor scenes with significant empty space, or when high resolution is required but memory is constrained [S7, S8, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Voxel grids are a fundamental representation in computer graphics and scientific visualization, dating back to the early days of 3D rendering. They provide an explicit discretization of 3D space into cubes, storing metadata in an array [S7, unofficial]. While simple, their $O(N^3)$ storage requirements have led to the development of sparse variants and neural representations like NeRF, which exploit symmetries that voxel grids do not [S7, unofficial]. Recent work, such as Direct Voxel Grid Optimization, has revisited dense voxel grids for neural rendering by optimizing the grid values directly, leveraging their fast query capabilities [S10].
+Voxel grids represent one of the earliest and most naive approaches to volume encoding, explicitly discretizing 3D space into cubes and storing metadata in an array [S7, unofficial]. The method relies on the fundamental computer graphics principle of mapping multi-dimensional coordinates to 1D linear memory via row-major strides [G]. While simple, the $O(N^3)$ storage requirement has driven the development of sparse voxel octrees and neural radiance fields, which exploit spatial symmetries and sparsity to overcome these limitations [S7, unofficial]. Modern applications, such as Direct Voxel Grid Optimization, revisit dense voxel grids by optimizing them directly as learnable parameters for fast neural rendering [S10].
 
